Z||000001||rogergarrison||03-02-2001||06:09 AM||||ask FieroLisa. all you have to really do is take out side sill trim, seats, center console. Just drop it in. Put everything back. It took her less than a couple hours.||||reg||1
Z||000002||PatrickB||03-02-2001||09:28 AM||||I did it with some black carpet that I got from JC Whitney. Took 2 packages of their car carpet.  <P>It only takes a few hours. All you have to do is take the seats out and pull up the carpet.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tina||03-02-2001||06:13 PM||||Did you guy's take the old carpet out?<BR>Any glue involved?<P>Just ordered mine a couple of days ago.<BR>So this is definitly in my future.<P>Tina||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||03-02-2001||06:59 PM||||NO GLUE needed unless you put new on door bottoms. Floor just lays there, held in by sill trim panel, center console and seats. in rear just lays under the back firewall cover, front just pokes up under the dash<P>go to FieroLisas website, she took pics of her job as she went.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroLisa||03-02-2001||07:35 PM||||yep definitely have pics on my website <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/interior.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/interior.html</A>  , it was one of the easiest things with the interior to do! If you have any more questions, you can email me but its really straightforward.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eric||03-02-2001||10:22 PM||||Isn't that just new old carpet?  I bet it would fit pretty nicely; it's made for the car by pontiac.  For the rest of us who want a color not originally offered by the manufacturer, results rarely turn out as well.  My carpet, for instance, is from JC Whitney.  It is black, and doesn't fit worth a crap.  I've installed it about a dozen times and still it doesn't look right.  I've got cable ties holding the edges to the consul and glue holding the new carpet to the old carpet.  If I were to do it again, I would buy the best quality auto carpet I could find with a high pile (if that's the correct term).  I would cut the carpet to fit over the original carpet perfectly instead of relying on some goofball who probably drives a ricksha to work.  Then I'd sew up the back of the new carpets and throw things together.  It would certainly be nicer than what I have now.  I'd love to show you guys some pictures of my interior before you make the unwise investment in new carpeting from JC Whitney or another company, who probably gets their carpet from the same contractor, but I can't figure out pip.  Or maybe I just don't want to.  Either way if you're interested just email me and I get some digital pics back to you.<P>I like the door panels done up in vinyl though.  If I redo my interior, it will include red vinyl door panels and red Mr. Mikes seats.  The rest will be black with the carbon fiber overlays, cause they're cool.  Of course they glare a lot, but that's the price you pay  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroLisa||03-02-2001||10:45 PM||||Eric- thats one of the reasons I kept with stock gray carpet- it may not look as nice as plain black, but then again it does since it actually fits right. And all I've heard about aftermarket carpet is complaints, if not about its looks, then about how hard it was to put in.<BR>Like I said, stock was easy- take out 4 bolts in the seat (which is easy by moving the seat forward and back to get to the them), taking the seat out, and then just lifting the carpet out...and using that padding under old carpet on new carpet too. Easy!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Eric||03-02-2001||11:03 PM||||My carpet looks like $hit.  I hate it!  They sewed in a heel pad, but it's in the wrong place so when I shift, my shoe catches the edge of the mat and I slip the clutch.  I might try cutting that out tommorow.  Did you find new sunvisors?  I got my old ones out of the car now pending "operation reline".  I've been cruising around without them for over a year.  Never realized how much I would miss them.||||reg||1
Z||000009||gabriel||03-02-2001||11:53 PM||||&gt;Like I said, stock was easy- take out 4 bolts in the seat (which is easy by moving the seat forward and back to get to the them), taking the seat out, and then just lifting the carpet out<P> Grumble, grumble, grouse, grouse ...<BR>I'll bet green money your Fiero doesn't have a sunroof.<P>  Mine does, & the prior owner did zero maintentence, (the roof leaked) & every nut had to be split, then drilled out, then the studs had to be rethreaded, etc, etc.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  <P>  Other than all that crap, it was easy.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>However the new carpet looks great!  I would never even consider laying new carpet over the old stuff.   <P>  Pay a few $$$ more for the good molded carpet -you'll be glad you did.||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroLisa||03-03-2001||12:24 AM||||gabriel- how much green money were you talking about, because mine does have a sunroof! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> difference was, mine was first owned by a pontiac mechanic who took good care of it, then in a garage for the next 6 years of its life, till now.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||gabriel||03-03-2001||12:53 AM||||&gt;gabriel- how much green money were you talking about, because mine does have a sunroof!  difference was, mine was first owned by a pontiac mechanic who took good care of it, then in a garage for the next 6 years of its life, till now.<P>  Oh heck, I've lost another!  LOL   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Now, if only every Fiero was always the recipient of such care ...<P>  Mine was owned by someone who disconnected or removed each system as it failed.  Including the drivers side window that was broken out!  No, he didn't remove the already missing glass, he just used duct tape & plastic for a year!  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||03-03-2001||09:38 AM||||I used an aftermarket carpet. Its solid brown as in a real Ferrari. It come molded for $135 in plush pile. It fit just like the OEM take out. Just had to trim the outside edge before i put the sill panel back on, and cut holes for seat bolts.  Still no glue anywhere. Took me all of 1 1/2 hours.<P>BTW Eric, not doggin you or anything, but I never buy anything from JC Whitney. Nothing i ever saw or got from them years ago ever worked or looked like it should. Suggest you throw your catalog away. Anything in there can be found elsewhere thats much better.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 03-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Eric||03-03-2001||10:53 AM||||Roger, was the carpet molded not sewn?  And where did you find it.  I think I paid around $90 for mine, so $135 for something that works, is not that far off base.  So I am to understand that you no longer have the original carpet in your car.  The new carpet had sound deadening and foam to fit all the ridges and valleys of the Fiero floor?  Can I come see it sometime?<P>Carpet was the only and last thing I've ordered from JC Whitney.||||reg||1
Z||000014||gabriel||03-03-2001||12:47 PM||||Here's one place that has the Fiero carpet:<P>          <A HREF="http://www.accmats.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.accmats.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||rogergarrison||03-03-2001||02:07 PM||||yes Eric, no original left. I get it thru AMZEE Wholesale Auto Upolstery. I dont have a phone book here right now but Its on Refugee Rd on Columbus south side just east of Lockbourne Rd. They may not sell to you retail, but I can order it for you thru shop. They had lots of different colors, plush and cut loop pile.||||reg||1
Z||000016||William||03-03-2001||09:55 PM||||Last weekend, I installed carpet that was purchased from ACC. I am extremely pleased. the carpet looks great and the fit was perfect. It looks like factory carpet only better. I went with light Camel tan cut pile to go with the buckskin leather, and the contrast with the black dash and consoles is awesome. I recommend this carpet. I'm getting up early tommorow to finish up the carpet panels for the door and shall post pics for all!||||reg||1
